const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens and wolves get along?', a: 'Ravens and wolves have a well-documented mutualistic relationship. Ravens follow wolf packs to access carcasses, while wolves\' hunting creates the carrion that ravens depend on in winter. In Yellowstone, ravens have been observed alerting wolves to carcasses they have found by flying toward the wolves and calling. Ravens play with wolf pups, engage in apparent games of tag with adult wolves, and tolerate close approach from wolves at carcasses — far more than they would tolerate from most other carnivores. The relationship is not merely exploitation; it appears to have genuine mutualistic elements.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens get along with dogs?', a: 'Ravens show a range of responses to domestic dogs — from bold opportunism (stealing food from dogs, playing near dogs) to avoidance. In areas where ravens have learned that dogs are associated with humans and food (farms, campsites), they may approach closely. Wild ravens have been documented playing with domesticated dogs, particularly in winter when other social stimulation is reduced. Ravens raised by humans sometimes form strong affiliations with dogs and cats in the household, treating them as social companions. The raven-dog relationship in rural communities is long-established — dogs and ravens both scavenge from the same human waste and carcasses.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens bother other birds?', a: 'Ravens are dominant over most other birds at food sources owing to their large size. They displace smaller corvids (crows, jackdaws, magpies), gulls, and raptors from carcasses. Ravens are mobbed by crows and smaller corvids but are generally undeterred. At breeding season, ravens occasionally prey on eggs and nestlings of other birds, particularly ground-nesters. They have complex relationships with large raptors (golden eagles): ravens mob eagles persistently, and the two species are both competitors for large carcasses. Ravens and eagles have been observed in what appear to be play interactions (aerial chasing) in some field accounts.' },
];
